00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.853
on the nature of Eric's Cautionary Tale.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.090
Okay, so in the big picture,

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.300
what was Eric's key message yesterday?

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.450
Well, I think it was that
causal inferences in complex

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:38.090
nonlinear systems is more
difficult than we wish it was.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.930
And it's certainly more difficult

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:43.140
than is convenient for us in policymaking.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.340
And when we are engaged in it,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.600
we're vulnerable to mistakes.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:48.660
And this has been shown

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.543
in climate science and in epidemiology.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:56.136
So one of the first
examples that Eric used

00:06:56.136 --> 00:07:01.136
was from climate science,
where between 1998 and 2012,

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.640
there was this observation of what seemed

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.160
to be a hiatus in global warming.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.160
And as Eric described,
scientists were eager to advance

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.270
many different hypotheses
about what was causing this.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:20.270
And as I've done here, we might
visualize these hypotheses

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.800
as the more attention grabbing
characters at a party.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.510
But the moral of Eric's
story was that, you know,

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.980
it's not always the attention grabbing

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.750
like policies that are
the correct ones, right?

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.519
And in the case of this observed
hiatus and global warning,

00:07:38.519 --> 00:07:42.199
the best explanation was
actually a discreet character,

00:07:42.199 --> 00:07:44.603
ordinary internal variability.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.530
So during COVID-19 many
scientists have turned

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:54.720
to the question of which
population level interventions

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.270
have been effective in
curbing transmission.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:02.340
And I took the upshot of
Eric's talk to be that

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:06.750
there's no good evidence
right now that any of these

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.430
have been effective and we
don't know how effective

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:10.943
if they have been, right?

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.408
So once again, the observed differences

00:08:14.408 --> 00:08:17.343
could be the result of
this discreet character.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.153
- Right.

00:08:19.153 --> 00:08:21.570
Stephanie, I just want to do, and this,

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.680
one of the clarification here
for boom, both you and Eric.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.480
And Eric this is, you know,

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.040
other than complete lockdown, right?

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.510
So that was clear.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:35.240
Would you agree that the,
what both of you agree that

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:40.240
complete lockdown prevented
transmission, right?

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.750
- I'm going to tell you my interpretation

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.310
was that has not been shown well.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:47.670
- Okay, great, thanks.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.496
I just wanted to in slack there.

00:08:49.496 --> 00:08:52.184
We don't come to that question.

00:08:52.184 --> 00:08:54.392
I want to make you.

00:08:54.392 --> 00:08:55.410
- Okay, and I think I have a little quote

00:08:56.313 --> 00:08:57.961
from a paper that does
go into depth about that.

00:08:57.961 --> 00:08:58.980
And we can definitely bring
that up in the discussion.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.790
I certainly did not read,
Eric's talk as being, you know,

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:04.105
that that even that has been shown.

00:09:04.105 --> 00:09:08.210
But Eric's talk gives us this
chance to go through some of

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.320
the challenges that come up
in addressing these types of

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.580
questions broadly, right?

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:17.730
So very often in
population health research,

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:21.100
the questions don't lend
themselves to randomization.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.860
They don't lend themselves
to randomized study designs

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.000
and that's either because
they're not ethical

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.150
or just because they're not feasible.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:32.060
So we need another way to
control for confounding.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.340
If we're trying to do causal inference.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:40.290
Now one big class of techniques
basically tries to exploit

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:45.290
some source of arbitrary variation
in who gets the treatment

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:46.770
and who doesn't.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.970
Sort of like across jurisdictions

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.810
across different regions and times.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:55.300
So Eric focused on two
such methods yesterday.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.980
The first being the difference
in differences method

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.590
and the second being synthetic control.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:04.598
So with the DID methods,
basically we're trying to identify

00:10:04.598 --> 00:10:08.950
sort of a natural treatment
group, natural control group,

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.910
like a place that
implemented a mask policy

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.740
and one that didn't.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.961
And then, you know, we're
trying to compare them

00:10:14.961 --> 00:10:18.130
in terms of the change in the outcome

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:22.010
before and after the
intervention was implemented.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.540
And then with synthetic control methods.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.605
The focus is similar,
but instead of using one

00:10:27.605 --> 00:10:32.100
or two specific real world control groups,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.180
there's a data-driven method
for stitching together,

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.510
numerous control groups
into one in a synthetic way.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:41.630
So basically identifying
multiple control units

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:44.120
that the analyst thinks, you know,

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.110
had similar characteristics,

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.810
had a similar outcome trajectory prior

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.544
to the intervention, similar
to the treatment group,

00:10:51.544 --> 00:10:53.873
and then doing a weighted average.

00:10:54.712 --> 00:10:58.720
So manage of the difference
in differences method

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:02.810
is that in theory, it's able
to account for confounding

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.340
between treatment and control,

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.020
as long as con founders
are fixed over time.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.270
But the flip side is that you
have to be able to assume that

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:12.730
there's no sudden changes in confounders.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.361
So as Eric explained,

00:11:14.361 --> 00:11:17.270
this is called the
parallel trends assumption.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.250
And it basically means that
we have to assume that,

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.530
you know, without the
intervention, the outcomes

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:24.890
for the treated and the
control groups, you know, would

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:28.243
have been following parallel
to a trajectory over time.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:32.260
Now the synthetic control, you know,

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.920
some reason I suddenly can't advance guy.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.670
Do you know why that would
be I suddenly can't advance?

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:41.753
Oh, I think I may because someone
going in the waiting room.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:43.523
Any suggestion?

00:11:43.523 --> 00:11:46.730
- And I admitted that person let's see.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.380
Okay, maybe try now.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:50.240
- Yeah, okay, now I'm good.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.150
- That's okay.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:55.260
- Okay, so synthetic control
have several requirements.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:56.310
I'm not going to go through them.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.500
They're all detailed in the Abadie article

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.011
that Eric circulated.

00:12:00.011 --> 00:12:03.840
But the point that Eric was
emphasizing yesterday was that

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.840
among the requirements,
there are two in particular

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.630
that are sort of
intention with each other.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:10.900
So first of all,

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:14.380
there needs to be appropriate
comparators available.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.540
So to add into this synthetic donor pool,

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:19.960
there need to be available
competitors that are, you know,

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.560
they have similar attributes
to the treatment group.

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:26.860
Now, second of all, there
can't be interference

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:30.150
in the sense of spill over
effects of the intervention.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.540
So there can't be sort of a phenomenon

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:33.903
where people are watching
and they're seeing

00:12:33.903 --> 00:12:36.020
that the intervention
is happening elsewhere,

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:39.070
and they're sort of taking it
upon themselves to do it too.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:42.340
So yesterday Eric put
up a quote from Abadie

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.260
and I'll do the same here.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:47.360
So viewers can maybe pause the
video and read this quote for

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.140
a good explanation summary

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:51.033
of this tension we're talking about.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.720
Okay, so in a nutshell,

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.445
I think Eric was emphasizing
yesterday that methods

00:12:58.445 --> 00:13:01.460
for causal inference have requirements.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.821
And it's risky business to be pretending

00:13:03.821 --> 00:13:06.574
that we satisfy those
requirements when we don't.

00:13:06.574 --> 00:13:08.493
So that was the takeaway, I think.

00:13:08.493 --> 00:13:13.493
And I think we might
understand this in terms

00:13:13.677 --> 00:13:17.960
of epistemic limitations,
which is a big picture problem

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:19.910
in population health research.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:23.270
And I want to connect this
to an article from last year

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:25.720
from a group led by Math Bay.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.230
This was a review of methods
that are commonly used

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:31.690
in population health research,
and they clustered them

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.480
into two main strategies.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:36.370
So these are these
instrument based approaches,

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.092
like the ones that Eric was
talking about yesterday.

00:13:39.092 --> 00:13:41.330
And then there's the methods

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.791
that are known as confounder
control approaches.

00:13:44.791 --> 00:13:48.496
And their conclusion was
ultimately that, you know,

00:13:48.496 --> 00:13:52.790
all of these methods rely
in one way or another

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.520
on some unverifiable assumptions.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.890
And further to that there
are trade-offs involved

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.270
in choosing what method to use.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:05.360
And ultimately having different
lines of evidence from

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:08.573
different study designs is
probably the best that we can do.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.490
They also made another note,
which I think is important,

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:15.907
which is that in their experience,

00:14:15.907 --> 00:14:18.490
researchers in population health,

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:22.530
they tend to rely on one
or the other method, right?

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.690
And there's often a lack of understanding

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.760
of the other method, right?

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.240
Whatever method one does not specialize,

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.940
there's often a lack of
familiarity with how it works.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:37.770
And this can be problematic
because to really interpret

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.820
the evidence well in population health,

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:43.100
one needs to understand the
trade-offs that are inherent

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.350
in the different methods.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.450
Okay, so there's
limitations in our methods

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:54.600
but there are also limitations
in our capacities, right?

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.120
And I want to connect this to a paper

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:58.070
that I think is relevant.

00:14:58.070 --> 00:15:00.900
I know it's on Eric's radar.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:04.610
So this is a critique
that came out of a model

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.600
that was focusing on the efficacy

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.890
of different COVID interventions
or, you know, NPI, right?

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:14.940
And, you know, these authors, right?

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:18.130
You know, we conclude that
the model is in effect too

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:20.560
flexible, and therefore allows the data

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.630
to be explained in various ways.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.960
And this has led the authors
to go beyond the data

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.100
in reporting that particular interventions

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.220
are especially effective.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.680
And they say this kind of error,

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:36.010
mistaking assumptions
for conclusions is easy

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:39.240
to make and not especially easy to catch.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.490
So that's a point I want
to put on the table.

00:15:42.856 --> 00:15:47.856
Okay, so moving on to
the so now what segment,

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.940
I think there are a number
of different directions

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.290
that we could take this conversation.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.380
And that includes ideas

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:58.040
like how can we overcome
these limitations?

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.630
But instead of doing that
today, I want to steer

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.970
towards some normative
questions because I think

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.130
they're relate to a number of
themes that we see coming out

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:09.010
in Eric's recent work, and
particularly the three papers

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.910
that he circulated in advance of the talk.

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.540
So on the bottom of the
screen here, I've put those

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.043
sort of truncated titles
of those three papers.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.500
And I just want to
distill a few key messages

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.180
from all of those papers.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:28.180
So first of all, scientific
models, broadly construed.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:32.010
They're not sort of
truth apt things, right?

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.226
They're evaluated in terms
of adequacy for purpose.

00:16:35.226 --> 00:16:40.226
And this emphasizes that modeling
is a value-laden activity,

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.610
both because purposes are evaluated

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:46.003
and so our notions of adequacy,

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:50.760
so there's a corresponding risk there.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.514
And that's the risk
that modeling decisions,

00:16:53.514 --> 00:16:57.930
that come up in the modeling
process and ultimately

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:02.010
models themselves are going
to be inadequate for purpose.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:06.060
Now, Eric and I call this
representational risk.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:09.460
And we distinguished
it from inductive risk,

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:13.920
which we take to be the risk
of endorsing a false fact.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:17.880
So the argument there is
that there's an advantage

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.750
to understanding those risks as different,

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.810
even though we might want to
draw connections between them.

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:26.730
And even though they both
give us insight into the role

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.273
of values and modeling
and values in science.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:35.880
Okay, so third Eric has argued
alongside some coauthors

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.220
that in certain contexts,
our epistemic standards

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.370
should actually be quite high.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:48.090
So for example, when it comes
to restricting civil liberties

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:51.870
it might be acceptable to
do this just on the basis

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.690
of precautionary reasoning
for a certain amount of time.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:59.750
But after a certain point,
this ceases to be acceptable.

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:01.720
And in the long run,

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:04.520
governments are only
justified in restricting civil

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.140
liberties on the basis of
good scientific evidence.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:11.030
So not just, you know, the
best available evidence,

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:15.590
but good scientific evidence,
and also on a careful

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:19.240
and transparent, you know,
cost effectiveness analysis,

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:22.103
which of course is another
normative enterprise.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:26.170
And then the last point, you know,

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:29.970
in the cost of learning
paper that he circulated,

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:33.330
Eric offers up a framework
to help us think about

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:35.890
the expected net benefits of research.

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:38.830
And this is taking into
consideration that, you know,

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:42.710
monetary costs of research are
not the only relevant costs.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:46.400
So Eric uses the term on
goody and costs to describe

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:49.790
sort of non-monetary costs of research.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.810
And he's given us some reasons
to think that under some

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:57.540
conditions research could have
a negative expected utility.

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.690
So two specific examples UnGoodian costs

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.634
that I think are relevant for today.

00:19:02.634 --> 00:19:06.410
The first is the
possibility that the results

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:10.043
of research might be taken
as being stronger evidence

00:19:10.043 --> 00:19:11.800
than we think is right.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:16.350
And so basically if we think
that decision-makers are going

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.990
to effectively misinterpret the evidence,

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:22.670
then research could have a
negative expected utility for us.

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:26.120
And further to that,
another UnGoodian cost

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:29.320
might be something like
negative externalities.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:32.620
So for example, maybe
research has been a signal

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:34.870
or something that we
don't want it to signal.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:37.380
And if that's the case, again,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:40.080
maybe research could have a
negative expected utility.

00:19:42.958 --> 00:19:46.030
I think I'm going to have my same slide

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:47.313
advancing problem folks.

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:50.830
Oh, there we go.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:55.572
Okay, so now that we know these
terms, that you're abuses,

00:19:55.572 --> 00:19:58.870
I just want to sort of riff
off of them and present an

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:01.748
imaginary scenario to
get us thinking about

00:20:01.748 --> 00:20:03.983
some of these normative questions.

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.220
Okay, so first for the sake of argument,

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.610
let's just imagine a model.

00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:11.730
Where we all know that the requirements

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:13.840
for causal inference are not met.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:17.280
So the model is not adequate
for causal inference.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:19.220
Now, interestingly,

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:23.950
it seems like one way to
evade representational risk

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:27.270
is to just say that the model
has a different purpose.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.140
So I'm thinking of something
a bit analogous to something

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:35.140
that we've seen in COVID
modeling, which is to basically

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:39.040
say, we know, well, the
model wasn't for prediction,

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:41.670
it was for conditional projection.

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:44.720
So then if a model said
something like, you know,

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.650
under conditions, A, B, C,

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:50.593
we see X number of infections
or X number of deaths,

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.530
but then those conditions don't
materialize those outcomes

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:57.284
don't materialize in the world.

00:20:57.284 --> 00:21:01.180
There seems to be no grounds
upon which we can object.

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.390
So when it comes to
models that don't satisfy

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.348
the requirements for causal inference.

00:21:05.348 --> 00:21:07.700
I think maybe we can imagine something

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:10.450
or more than one thing that
would be analogous to that.

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:15.720
So for me, a few distinct possibilities,

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:19.450
come to mind if we go ahead
and we build this model.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:23.600
So first the model gets
genuinely misinterpreted.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:25.700
It gets misinterpreted in good faith,

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:29.890
maybe by policy makers or
scientists from another discipline

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:33.060
who thinks that causal
inference is what it's for.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:34.453
And they use it for that.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.690
Second option, maybe the model gets used

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:40.380
as a rhetorical device.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.780
So no one actually thinks the model

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:46.700
can support causal inference,
but doing it anyway,

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:50.170
allows people to advance
their own preferred policies.

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:52.223
And so they use the model to this end.

00:21:54.128 --> 00:21:54.961
And then third,

00:21:54.961 --> 00:21:57.360
we might imagine that the
model just goes unused.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.550
So in other words, everyone
recognizes the models,

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:02.720
not for causal inference, causal inference

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.460
is what they want and
so they just move on.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.910
So I think at the very least,

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:10.454
there's probably some waste
of money involved there,

00:22:10.454 --> 00:22:13.600
but there might be some
negative externalities too.

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:16.693
So maybe in terms of what
we signal to the public.

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:21.620
And then finally, you know,
since we're imagining this,

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.760
I think we need to imagine
a few contextual factors

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:26.366
that are probably relevant.

00:22:26.366 --> 00:22:28.820
So the first that comes
to my mind would be

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.770
the degree of that systemic capacity.

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:35.400
So to what degree is
it possible to know all

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:38.530
of the epistemically relevant
elements of this model?

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:40.820
Maybe that degree of epistemic opacity

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:43.453
would change the picture
for us in some way.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:50.260
Second, to what extent are
various agents directly engaging

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.880
with the model and doing
their own assessments

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:55.110
of adequacy for purpose?

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:57.320
Because it seems like in many contexts,

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.620
it's actually a relatively
small number of people who are

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:04.240
really looking under the hood
of the model at a relatively

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:05.530
large number of people

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:08.290
who are just relying on epistemic trust.

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:11.366
And in fact, it seems to be a
standard feature of current,

00:23:11.366 --> 00:23:13.994
you know, epistemically
distributed research

00:23:13.994 --> 00:23:16.523
and policy-making context.

00:23:17.955 --> 00:23:22.750
Now three, assuming that this
is a policy oriented model,

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:27.650
to what extent do people agree
or disagree about what counts

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:28.760
as a benefit?

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:31.887
So this seems relevant,
particularly in contexts,

00:23:31.887 --> 00:23:34.370
you know, like nurse mandates,

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:38.530
it seems like people deeply
disagree about what should be

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:41.990
counted as a cost and what
should be counted as a benefit

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:43.490
when it comes to wearing masks.

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:48.070
So for example, if mask
wearing affords me a level of

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:51.583
perceived comfort, does that
make it on the ledger or not.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:55.760
Beyond that there seems
to be some disagreement

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:58.630
about what price we should be prepared

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:01.260
to pay for what level of benefit.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:05.340
So even for example, let's
say we had perfect knowledge

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:10.340
that masks cause a 5%
reduction in COVID infections.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:13.510
Would that mean that we should
implement a mask policy?

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:15.536
So it seems like there's at least

00:24:15.536 --> 00:24:19.343
not good agreement there or
not good evidence of agreement.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:23.180
Finally, I think we should
just get it on the table that,

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:24.013
you know, again,

00:24:24.013 --> 00:24:27.940
people do say we need complimentary
evidence from different

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:31.740
study designs to support
robust causal inferences.

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.380
So maybe there are some of us
who would carve out a role for

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:39.160
models that they don't allow
causal inference themselves,

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:42.067
but maybe in some way they
would help us reason things out,

00:24:42.067 --> 00:24:43.920
down the road maybe,

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.270
or possibly in combination
with something else.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:51.880
Okay, so this leads me to pose
Eric a few direct questions

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:55.532
and maybe he can pick, you
know, which ones interest him.

00:24:55.532 --> 00:24:58.980
So first, you know what I'd like to know,

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.900
if you think it's legitimate
to assign a causal model,

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:05.983
a different purpose to
evade representational risk.

00:25:07.017 --> 00:25:10.930
Also, do you think that there
are conditions under which,

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:13.300
you know, models of complex linear systems

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.623
would maybe have a
negative expected utility?

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:19.670
Finally, sort of a two-part
question, you know,

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:23.306
can we do a better job of
articulating epistemic standards

00:25:23.306 --> 00:25:26.350
in modeling for causal inference?

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:28.180
And then what about at
the systemic standards

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:30.320
in the policy making context?

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:31.658
So on the latter points,

00:25:31.658 --> 00:25:34.358
I am thinking about a couple
of things in particular.

00:25:34.358 --> 00:25:36.810
I'm wondering, you know,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.130
do we need to specify the
model purpose in advance?

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.646
Does it need to be done in a
particular level of detail?

00:25:43.646 --> 00:25:45.220
Do we need to spell out

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:47.680
what are legitimate purposes for models?

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:51.100
And do we need to set
standards around who should be

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:52.730
involved in determining

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:54.973
whether a model is adequate for purpose?

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:59.470
So those are just some ideas
to get the ball rolling.

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:01.130
These are the references that I mentioned,

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.970
if anyone would like them and
I will toss it over to Eric.

00:26:07.550 --> 00:26:10.670
- Thank you so much, Stephanie,

00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:12.390
for these wonderful comments.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:13.243
Eric please.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:16.780
- Slide again.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:20.083
- Oh yeah, I guess I have
to press share screen again.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.148
Is that convenient for everyone?

00:26:22.148 --> 00:26:25.131
- Right.

00:26:25.131 --> 00:26:26.010
- Is there any disadvantage

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.060
to my being the one that shares it again?

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.440
- Okay, so yeah.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:35.940
So the first question, I think it's tricky

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:40.578
because I think when you
do something like that

00:26:40.578 --> 00:26:43.850
so in other words, the idea
is like, could I take model

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:49.810
that, you know, looks like
it's job is to, you know,

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:52.840
attribute causes us to something.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:54.680
But there's not adequate for that.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:58.720
And I mitigate the damage
of that by being clear

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:01.237
that this is not what the model is for.

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:03.700
One thing this makes me think I'm just

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.763
maybe I'll back up to climate.

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:10.010
So in climate science, there's a kind

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:15.010
of long standing history of
these things called the RCPs

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:20.570
the representative carbon pathways.

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:23.270
I think what it stands for,
like in my head that round.

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:25.403
And there's RCP 4.5 and
RCP 6.5 and RCP 8.5.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:32.230
And pretty clearly these
pathways were picked, you know,

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:34.890
way back, I think in maybe the early 90s

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:36.540
maybe that far ago, I'm not sure.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:41.240
But there was the idea that RCP 4.5 was

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:43.530
often called business as usual.

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:44.530
And what it is, right?

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.170
It's all, it's just a project.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:50.050
It's just a graph of
carbon in the atmosphere

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:51.640
from, you know, the present

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:54.070
until the end of the 21st century.

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.580
And it's pretty standard
for, you know, climate models

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:01.580
to run projections on
these RCPs and show you,

00:28:02.623 --> 00:28:06.417
this is how hot the world
is going to be for RCP 4.5.

00:28:06.417 --> 00:28:10.190
So I'm not the world's going
to be RCP 6.5, et cetera.

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:12.510
Okay, so some people have sort of started

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:16.480
to complain a little bit
about the fact that RCP 8.5

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:20.623
is still often in the
toolkit, even though it looks.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:26.720
Even though it's not, sorry, what.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.773
I'm getting a right.

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:34.850
- Could I just request everyone else

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:37.300
to mute their microphones, please?

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:38.133
That might help.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:47.880
- I'm struggling to mute mine
'cause I'm stuck on share.

00:28:50.572 --> 00:28:52.020
- I don't think you're the problem.

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:55.370
- Really weird, I'm not one, okay.

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:57.144
- I don't know maybe not anyway.

00:28:57.144 --> 00:28:59.560
Good, so there's been
some people wondering

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:00.800
why are we still doing, you know,

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:05.010
why are we still doing 8.5,
given that it certainly seems

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:08.120
as though the world has
made enough progress,

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:11.472
that 8.5 doesn't really
look like it's in the cards.

00:29:11.472 --> 00:29:12.980
So in other words, you
know, we haven't had

00:29:14.692 --> 00:29:18.280
the massive increasing in
carbon output that, you know,

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.610
maybe people worried
would happen in the 90s

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:23.480
and that's represented in RCP 8.5.

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:25.437
And of course it's, you
know, a perfectly okay thing.

00:29:25.437 --> 00:29:27.060
And, you know, a lot of people
say that they say, look,

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:28.128
these are just pathways.

00:29:28.128 --> 00:29:29.440
These are just storylines.

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:33.000
You know, 8.5 is just a
story that we're not saying,

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:34.340
there's no indication here that this is

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:36.690
what's going to happen,
these are not predictions.

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:37.800
These are just projections.

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:40.170
This is just like, if we were to do 8.5,

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:42.170
this is what the world would like.

00:29:42.170 --> 00:29:44.780
I think there's, you know,
that there's, you know,

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:49.780
of all the things that you
get in the climate to be,

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:51.460
I think that's one where there's actually

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.673
some pretty honest debate
that we had about that, right?

00:29:55.550 --> 00:30:00.040
Why, you know, how confident are we to be,

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:07.123
that when we put things like
that into the public domain

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:11.900
for consumption that people
will understand this, right?

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:15.720
That 8.5 doesn't really look
like it's on the table anymore.

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:17.870
To what extent or should we be worried

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:19.530
that they're going to
think, look this is this,

00:30:19.530 --> 00:30:21.070
we know that some
vaguely in our, you know,

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:24.360
in the back of our heads 8.5
is so-called business as usual.

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:27.100
So when I see the projection on the graph

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:31.820
that's being circulated by
various climate scientists,

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:36.820
various labs, why not say

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:38.203
why isn't it reasonable for me to think

00:30:38.203 --> 00:30:43.203
that that highest curve is
what I will expect the world

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:48.790
to behave like if we don't,
you know, make major changes.

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:52.950
So, you know, that's a
tough question, right?

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:56.790
I think when you're putting a
model out there and, you know,

00:30:56.790 --> 00:31:00.020
handing to policymakers, not
just handing policymakers

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:02.400
would play it out there
for public consumption

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:06.610
as a way of, you know, and
I don't think it's stretched

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:08.760
to suggest that both climate scientists

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:11.530
and public health experts
over the last year have been,

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:15.220
you know, trying to
influence public perceptions

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:17.180
of what ought to be done.

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:19.900
I think there are interesting
questions there about what,

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:24.120
you know, whether it's legitimate
to put a model out there

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:28.020
that looks like it's doing A,
but in the fine print, right?

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.870
It's like, well, actually, if
we're not actually claiming

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:32.490
that we're making causal inferences here,

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:33.910
we're not actually claming anymore

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:37.230
that this is business as
usual, et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:40.130
You know, I do think there's
legitimate worries there

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:45.130
that you're now drifting
from your job as someone

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:52.270
who I take it, you know, the
standard line is your job

00:31:52.270 --> 00:31:56.620
as an academic expert is to
provide us with, you know,

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:58.583
the best information that we can get.

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:04.860
Your job is not necessarily
right to make a determination

00:32:08.540 --> 00:32:13.030
about what the best thing
to encourage, the best thing

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:16.440
to get people to support our policy,

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:20.750
because what the best thing to
support policy is, oh, yeah.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:23.480
You know, as we all
know, obviously, right?

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:28.480
A combination of what to believe
and what to value, right?

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:34.230
And so I think there's a
fine line to be walked there

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:39.810
about when, you know,
modeling efforts like that,

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:45.220
where something is a model is
inadequate for some purpose,

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:46.410
the fine print just says,

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:49.060
we're not really using it
for that for that purpose.

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:50.800
But it sure looks like, right?

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:53.080
Sure, it looks like for public consumption

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.570
that that's the purpose it's useful for.

00:32:55.570 --> 00:33:00.220
I think there are issues
there that, you know, sort of,

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:04.900
that's a possibly sneaky
way of reaching that line

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:09.900
between informing people and
sort of imposing your views

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:16.143
of what the best balance
of cost and benefits is.

00:33:18.586 --> 00:33:21.843
Are there conditions under
which models of complex

00:33:21.843 --> 00:33:25.443
and our systems have
negative expected utility?

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:29.893
Yeah, I mean, for sure
there could be, right?

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:34.600
I mean, the phrase on goody and costs

00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:38.980
that Stephanie mentioned,
it's just me kind of getting

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:41.840
a more formal decision
theoretic framework to a line

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:43.640
of argument that comes from Kitcher.

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:47.170
Kitcher was interested in
the question of, you know,

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:51.720
when is it bad to do
research into the, you know,

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:56.880
natural ability, natural
differences in affiliating

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:59.113
that exists between sexes and races.

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:01.530
And he just mounts the
argument that, you know,

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:04.240
this is generally speaking
research that's going to have

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:07.673
negative expected utility because right.

00:34:09.331 --> 00:34:12.030
Simply because, you know, when it looks

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:14.060
like there's evidence
that there's no difference

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:16.200
in abilities, people tend to ignore it.

00:34:16.200 --> 00:34:17.080
When it looks like there's evidence

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:19.830
that there is ability that
makes every newspaper,

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:23.750
it gets on, you know, and
every, it blows off everywhere.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:26.770
And so there's a kind of
much higher probability

00:34:26.770 --> 00:34:29.200
of that evidence being misinterpreted

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:31.190
in one direction than the other.

00:34:31.190 --> 00:34:34.290
And then there's a much
higher cost of being wrong.

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:35.400
He thinks right on research

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:37.050
and I agree with him that, right?

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:43.703
If there are differences
and you wrongly point out

00:34:43.703 --> 00:34:46.800
that there aren't probably
nothing's going to happen anyway.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:48.573
But if there are differences
and you point out

00:34:48.573 --> 00:34:50.450
that there are, you're going to cause

00:34:50.450 --> 00:34:52.010
an enormous amount of harm, right?

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.430
To people, by finding this out.

00:34:54.430 --> 00:34:59.430
So that's the kind of condition
under which like, you know,

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:02.940
and not like evidence gathering practice

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:05.423
can have negative expected utility.

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:09.450
You know, look over the last year,

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:13.250
we have engaged
interventions that have been

00:35:13.250 --> 00:35:17.403
enormously costly to a lot
of people in a lot of places.

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:23.060
You know, particularly there's
I think been a phenomenon

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.400
of the following kind, right?

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:29.400
Where, you know, public health experts

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:33.940
in developed countries,
particularly let's say

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:36.750
the United States and
the UK have been trying

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:40.420
to influence policy
here and putting forward

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:42.960
certain kinds of models
that showed certain,

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:44.923
you know, benefits to interventions.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:48.180
And then those interventions
have been tried

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.980
in developing countries.

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:54.220
I have in mind particularly
India and South Africa

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:59.910
where those interventions
were both wildly sustainable.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:02.010
So they had very low likelihood
of actually providing

00:36:02.010 --> 00:36:04.200
any benefit in the long run

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:05.890
and had just enormous cost, right?

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:07.980
So we know about these
things, like, you know,

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:09.900
these death marches in India that happened

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:13.360
when migrant workers were
just cut off from their jobs

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:16.500
and their housing and
their transportation.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:21.500
So, yeah, I do think that
when you engage in inference,

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:29.400
that you kind of know in advance
that you don't really have

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:34.130
good tools for getting reliable answers.

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:36.120
You ought to think hard
about what the positive

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:39.380
possible negative, expect
each of you just gathering

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:42.210
the evidence is, so I agree
with Kitcher about that.

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:46.650
Can we do a better job of articulating

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:50.360
epistatic standards modeling
for causal inference?

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:51.790
I mean, I think this is tricky.

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:53.100
And I think one of the
things that makes it

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:57.040
especially tricky is a difference between,

00:36:57.040 --> 00:36:58.912
this is going to be a muffle,

00:36:58.912 --> 00:37:01.150
the difference between
difference in difference

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.883
and synthetic control methods.

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:08.520
I think one of the features
of synthetic control methods

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:13.520
for causal inference is it
looks like a magic trick, right?

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:17.363
But if you read the sort
of foundational papers,

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:21.550
I guess I don't really know
how to pronounce his name,

00:37:21.550 --> 00:37:22.646
Abadie, is that funny like that?

00:37:22.646 --> 00:37:23.795
I'm not sure is that?

00:37:23.795 --> 00:37:24.860
- Abadie.

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:25.860
- Abadie, thank you.

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:28.420
If you read like his
foundational papers on this,

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:30.310
he's quite clear, right?

00:37:30.310 --> 00:37:35.160
That doing synthetic control
requires a lot of skill

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:39.670
on the part of the person
making the donor pool

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:41.530
for the synthetic control method.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:43.570
And then if you don't exercise great care

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:44.990
and choosing a donor pool,

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:47.593
you can show almost anything
causes anything else.

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:51.220
So I think, you know, that
difference in difference method

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:54.120
actually that synthetic control method has

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:56.340
that disadvantage relative
to, I guess, difference

00:37:56.340 --> 00:37:58.340
in different method
that it really requires

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:01.640
quite a bit more trust in the modeler.

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:07.570
It's a little bit harder to
look under the hood and see

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:09.890
whether things have been done

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:12.450
in a way that really does eliminate

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:14.373
the likelihood of compounds there.

00:38:16.330 --> 00:38:18.240
Yeah, and what about epistatic standards

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:19.750
and evidence of proposal making?

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:22.660
I mean, look, I think
we ought to be having

00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:24.450
a lot of conversations about this.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:29.450
I don't know what the magic
wand is, but I do think that,

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:35.770
you know, a lot of these
with all the reasons

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:40.720
we've been talking about,
a lot of these kinds

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:45.720
of scientific work that
informs policy can very quickly

00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:52.690
look like they are evidence
for a policy choice, right?

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:53.523
And I think if you go back and say,

00:38:53.523 --> 00:38:57.119
look at my report 90
by the SAGE group in UK

00:38:57.119 --> 00:38:59.650
that came out I think
March 19th 2020, right?

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:02.940
This was probably the most
influential scientific

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:05.300
report in the history of the world.

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:07.780
I don't think that's an exaggeration.

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:12.290
It very much reads like,
you know, an advocacy

00:39:12.290 --> 00:39:14.053
for a set of policy choices.

00:39:15.875 --> 00:39:18.190
And it's I think quite clear

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:21.710
that those policy choices
involved balances of costs

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:26.710
and benefits that for
whom the stakeholders were

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:28.703
literally everybody in the world.

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:34.800
So yeah, I think that
it's important that we,

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:39.050
in some way or other, and I
think we need to be having

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:42.080
a lot of conversations about
how the best way to do this as,

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:45.010
and I don't have the answers,
but in some way or other,

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:47.730
we need to be, I think
thinking about the ways

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:52.393
in which when research
like that has been done.

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:59.160
You know, that process of sort of the ways

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:01.650
in which values are entering

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:05.560
into the scientific inference itself are

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:08.320
in some way or another, you know,

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:11.750
getting at the representative values.

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:14.880
Because I think, you know,
one thing that I've sort

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:19.560
of had the growing feeling of
over the course of the last 14

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:22.830
or 16 or whatever months,
however many months it is now,

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:25.100
is that, you know, a lot of the policies

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:29.130
that we've been advocating
have been really good

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:33.410
for the kinds of people that
do the kind of scientific work

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:35.080
that goes behind the policy making.

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:38.171
I mean, there was the
stuff in science studies

00:40:38.171 --> 00:40:41.410
that was quite popular in the 1980s

00:40:41.410 --> 00:40:42.570
called interest modeling.

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:44.570
And the idea was like it
was in sociologist science.

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:49.240
The idea was you explain
every episode of the history

00:40:49.240 --> 00:40:52.110
of science in terms of like
the scientists just reached

00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:54.434
the conclusions that were good for them.

00:40:54.434 --> 00:40:55.610
And, you know, the classic case was

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:58.790
like 19th century Victorian
work into eugenics, right?

00:40:58.790 --> 00:41:03.790
That was like the typical
example of interest modeling.

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:07.200
And, you know, at the
time I didn't sort of,

00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:08.640
wasn't a big fan of that stuff

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:12.220
and in general I'm not necessarily,
I think fan of that way

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:15.270
of thinking about the history
of science in general.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:18.340
But it's felt pretty strongly
to me over the last year

00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:23.120
that that's, you know, that
we have instituted policies

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:26.570
over the last 16 months that
have effectively, you know,

00:41:26.570 --> 00:41:31.570
diverted the virus away towards
everybody, but, you know,

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:34.760
upper middle class knowledge workers.

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:39.003
And I don't think that's
a massive coincidence.

00:41:40.240 --> 00:41:44.640
So I think that's the money question.

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:47.260
Like Stephanie's last
question on the slide here,

00:41:47.260 --> 00:41:48.550
but I don't know what the answer is,

00:41:48.550 --> 00:41:53.470
but I definitely think this is
a crucial thing to be talking

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:57.880
about if science is going
to be informing policy is

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:02.010
how do we make sure that the
epistatic standards being used

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:03.283
in that scientific work?

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:06.530
You know, are representing the values

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:10.890
of all the stakeholders because
it's pretty clear to me,

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:13.813
at least that, you know, what we've seen

00:42:13.813 --> 00:42:17.510
over the last several months
has not been something

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:19.860
where, you know, the science
has been done in a kind

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:22.890
of cool this passionate
way that just, you know,

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:25.704
being informative so that
then policymakers can,

00:42:25.704 --> 00:42:27.660
that's our standard picture, right?

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:28.870
The scientists are
supposed to just give us

00:42:28.870 --> 00:42:31.800
the information and then the
policymakers are supposed

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:33.640
to represent the interests
of their stakeholders

00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:34.620
and combine those interests

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:37.200
with that knowledge and pick policy.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:40.010
That first part I don't think played out

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:40.843
that way this year.

00:42:40.843 --> 00:42:42.562
So I think it is time.

00:42:42.562 --> 00:42:44.560
I think it is, you know,
we need to be having

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:46.430
more and more of that conversation.

00:42:46.430 --> 00:42:49.060
Okay, I think I'll let other people jump

00:42:49.060 --> 00:42:50.160
into the conversation.

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:57.630
- Thank you so much, Eric
for that detailed sort

00:42:57.630 --> 00:42:59.520
of response and engagement with some

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:04.480
of the really important questions
that Stephanie has raised.

00:43:04.480 --> 00:43:09.180
So I think I wanted to take
the liberty of sort of opening

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:13.340
up to the floor with a question myself.

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:15.710
And, you know, I think
I wanted to come back

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:19.090
to this idea that both of
you had have touched upon,

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:22.510
which is the purposes of modeling, right.

00:43:22.510 --> 00:43:27.130
And clarity about the
purpose of modeling and that,

00:43:27.130 --> 00:43:31.010
it's almost that the
purpose of that you are

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:36.010
modeling either to make
predictions for practical reasons,

00:43:36.460 --> 00:43:39.820
in terms of resource management,
in the case of COVID,

00:43:39.820 --> 00:43:42.550
it was to ensure that,
you know, there are enough

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:44.970
hospital beds in particular
cities and regions.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:48.030
So that was sort of driving
that kind of resource

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.903
availability and
assessment was driving it.

00:43:52.170 --> 00:43:56.219
I was curious about
what you both felt about

00:43:56.219 --> 00:44:01.219
how do we present the motive for modeling

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:05.660
in a more clear cut, transparent way?

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:07.164
I mean, there are,

00:44:07.164 --> 00:44:09.820
of course always going to be
people with agendas who will

00:44:09.820 --> 00:44:13.270
want to read into it,
no matter how you state,

00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:15.060
what your purpose is,
how clearly you state,

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:16.277
what your purpose is.

00:44:16.277 --> 00:44:21.277
But I was wondering if you
both had some thoughts about,

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:24.410
you know, how could we have
a bit more transparency

00:44:25.770 --> 00:44:29.900
in at least the motives for,
you know, a particular model

00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:32.543
and the presentation of a
model in a particular way.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:35.990
- Eric, do you want to go first.

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:38.480
- Well, I thought maybe
you take that one first?

00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:41.010
- Okay, I do have some
thoughts on this matter,

00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:42.730
particularly in health economics,

00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:47.700
because very often people will
talk about models and health

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:51.160
economics as being for
the purpose of prediction.

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:55.140
And in fact, one of the
major guideline documents

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:59.280
about health economics modeling
has a line that happens

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:04.280
to get me a little worked
up, which is that ultimately

00:45:04.310 --> 00:45:07.510
what matters is that a model reproduces,

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:09.760
what happens in reality.

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:12.614
And I'm only paraphrasing
very slightly, right?

00:45:12.614 --> 00:45:17.020
And I think that in a lot of contexts,

00:45:17.020 --> 00:45:19.220
we're not going to get there, right?

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:21.740
And Eric, I imagined
that you agree, right?

00:45:21.740 --> 00:45:26.560
Because what we're trying to
do is predict a system that

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:29.930
probably defies prediction

00:45:29.930 --> 00:45:32.610
really at any meaningful level, right?

00:45:32.610 --> 00:45:35.029
Particularly in the
context of, for example,

00:45:35.029 --> 00:45:38.368
very often, you know,
particularly in Canada,

00:45:38.368 --> 00:45:42.330
we do two clinical trials
to evaluate a new drug.

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:46.240
And then we do simulation
models to try to anticipate

00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:48.205
what the cost and the
benefits are going to be

00:45:48.205 --> 00:45:49.890
to the health system of

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:52.780
making this drug available to the public.

00:45:52.780 --> 00:45:54.610
Now, we all know the limitations

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:56.830
of randomized controlled trials, right?

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:58.620
They're conducted under conditions

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:01.170
that are not like the ones
that exist in the real world.

00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:05.220
So to just imagine that you're
going to be able to take that

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:09.170
evidence and put it into a
simulation model and have a good

00:46:09.170 --> 00:46:11.710
grip on what's really going to happen.

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:15.600
I don't have a lot of
confidence in that process.

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:20.600
You know, further to
that, I think to be able

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:22.730
to make meaningful predictions, you'd have

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:25.630
to have a way of testing,
whether they can doubt, right?

00:46:25.630 --> 00:46:28.260
And we don't really do that, right?

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:29.350
In the Canadian healthcare system,

00:46:29.350 --> 00:46:31.270
once they invest in a particular drug,

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:33.528
they don't do rigorous
studies to say, okay, well,

00:46:33.528 --> 00:46:37.050
now that we've invested in
it, what has happened, right?

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:40.230
So in my own opinion, I
don't think it's quite right

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:43.280
to say that the purpose of
those models is prediction.

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:45.050
I don't think that's well put.

00:46:45.050 --> 00:46:47.430
I think it's better to
say that the purpose

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:50.680
of that model is to
inform decisions, right?

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:53.295
And I think the next step then
is to talk about under what

00:46:53.295 --> 00:46:57.270
conditions people are wanting
to make decisions and what

00:46:57.270 --> 00:46:59.480
their attitude is to uncertainty.

00:46:59.480 --> 00:47:02.363
Because there are some really
important normative questions

00:47:02.363 --> 00:47:04.170
that need to be answered there.

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:05.690
And there's the dominant line of thinking

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:08.640
in health economics that
basically says you should just

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:11.280
not mind uncertainty and you should go

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.770
with what looks like the best bet,

00:47:13.770 --> 00:47:16.670
turning a blind eye to the
uncertainty that you have.

00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:17.503
And, you know,

00:47:17.503 --> 00:47:19.700
some folks might agree that
that's the best way to do it.

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:22.410
I don't happen to agree, which
I think other scores that

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:24.809
that's actually kind of
a moral judgment, right?

00:47:24.809 --> 00:47:28.270
So I think that we need to
actually do a better job of

00:47:28.270 --> 00:47:29.671
articulating the purpose of the model,

00:47:29.671 --> 00:47:34.123
do a better job of fleshing
out our relevant attitudes,

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:38.610
and then having more public
debate around how we want

00:47:38.610 --> 00:47:41.090
to do this, because you
could probably do it in way

00:47:41.090 --> 00:47:45.050
more ways than we're ever
even getting on the table.

00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:45.883
- Right.

00:47:46.734 --> 00:47:48.927
- I just want to add
one quick thing to that,

00:47:48.927 --> 00:47:51.570
which is that I just don't
even get the idea that

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:55.350
the purpose of a policy
guiding model is prediction

00:47:55.350 --> 00:47:58.030
because after all policy
choices are always choices

00:47:58.030 --> 00:48:00.260
between at least two alternatives.

00:48:00.260 --> 00:48:02.817
And so just being able to
predict what happens under one

00:48:02.817 --> 00:48:06.620
particular policy, choice
is totally uninformative.

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:09.360
I need to know what would
have happened and the other

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:12.870
possible policy choice,
and that's not prediction.

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:15.760
And, you know, I mean, I
had a key debates on that,

00:48:15.760 --> 00:48:17.683
you know, sort of well-known,

00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:21.420
I get hysterical about the
imperial college model and report

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:24.210
nine and the safe group, and
some people like to point out,

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:25.870
oh, they actually predicted the course

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:28.680
of the epidemic rather
well in certain countries.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:29.513
Well, yes.

00:48:29.513 --> 00:48:31.770
I mean, they predicted it
really well in the countries

00:48:31.770 --> 00:48:34.540
that followed their advice
because in their model,

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:38.120
they, you know, vastly
overstated the fatality

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:41.080
of the virus and vastly
overstated the effectiveness

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:43.380
of the parcels that they recommended.

00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:46.310
So obviously in places where
people followed exactly their

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:49.540
policy, those canceled out and
their predictions were great.

00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:51.440
But the predictions for
let's say, you know,

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:55.362
Sweden or for Florida and
were bad in their predictions

00:48:55.362 --> 00:48:58.390
for developing countries were horrible.

00:48:58.390 --> 00:49:00.160
And it's just, it's not
just saying, oh, well,

00:49:00.160 --> 00:49:02.210
we predicted what was going to
happen in the UK was going to

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:06.380
be well that's totally uninformative.

00:49:06.380 --> 00:49:10.800
I want to know when
you're full spectrum of

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:13.630
counterfactual projections
laws, because that's

00:49:13.630 --> 00:49:16.230
what informs policy predictions
don't inform policy.

00:49:17.890 --> 00:49:20.060
But yeah and then I.

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:23.850
- Yeah, so I think thank you both.

00:49:23.850 --> 00:49:24.853
I think that they,

00:49:26.660 --> 00:49:31.160
perhaps one way to have seen
these predictions that were

00:49:31.160 --> 00:49:36.160
made is simply seeing them
as maps of possibilities.

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:38.250
Maps of possibilities and, you know,

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:42.510
and I think the problem with
narratives public narratives

00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:45.750
is that because people
don't really understand

00:49:45.750 --> 00:49:49.120
what uncertainty inherent
uncertainty means and what a lack

00:49:49.120 --> 00:49:52.610
of knowledge of evolving sort
of the provisional nature

00:49:52.610 --> 00:49:54.950
of wheel figuring stuff
out about the virus itself

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:59.087
was hindering us from
really getting a grip.

00:49:59.087 --> 00:50:02.880
I think that, you know, what
might've been useful was like,

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:07.114
you know, a map of potential scenarios.

00:50:07.114 --> 00:50:10.460
And then discuss policy interventions

00:50:10.460 --> 00:50:13.920
in the context of a range
of these projections.

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:17.630
And not as you all said to
fixate on one and say, well,

00:50:17.630 --> 00:50:20.345
how can we, but I wanted to come back

00:50:20.345 --> 00:50:22.910
because Stephanie brought this up.

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:25.670
You know, a little bit of clarification.

00:50:25.670 --> 00:50:28.050
And from your experience expertise,

00:50:28.050 --> 00:50:31.720
my impression as a person
who works in a field where,

00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:34.200
you know, we don't really do
randomized controlled trials,

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:35.460
but, you know, I sort of follow it

00:50:35.460 --> 00:50:38.500
because they are seen as
the gold standard and so on.

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:40.600
And in astronomy, there
are people who aspire

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:45.600
to do randomized control styles
in terms of selecting image,

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:49.630
identification, and classification
and so on and so forth.

00:50:49.630 --> 00:50:51.820
But to the technique itself,

00:50:51.820 --> 00:50:56.820
isn't it true that the whole
methodology zooms that you have

00:50:59.450 --> 00:51:04.020
independent non interleaved
causes and that you can

00:51:04.020 --> 00:51:08.253
therefore isolate them if
you have compound causes.

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:13.980
And if you don't know what
all causes are possible,

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:15.610
I mean, our cities are no good, right?

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:18.050
I mean, I was just curious, you know,

00:51:18.050 --> 00:51:20.040
what are really seen are,

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:22.330
do people in health in
particular because, you know,

00:51:22.330 --> 00:51:24.720
that's where these are
sort of the gold standard.

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:26.990
Are the limitations of these techniques

00:51:26.990 --> 00:51:30.273
also being sort of actively studied now.

00:51:31.270 --> 00:51:33.840
- I think definitely the
limitations of our CTS

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:37.750
are more and more tended to.

00:51:37.750 --> 00:51:40.840
And in particular I think
the language gold standard

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:42.100
is definitely on the way out.

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:44.590
I think there are definitely
people that, you know,

00:51:44.590 --> 00:51:48.410
still use that but, and I could
be mixing up my fields here.

00:51:48.410 --> 00:51:50.960
I know philosophers of science
do not use that term anymore

00:51:50.960 --> 00:51:53.026
and they have good
reasons for not using it.

00:51:53.026 --> 00:51:55.060
So there are again, yeah,

00:51:55.060 --> 00:51:57.550
like a few assumptions
that we have to go on.

00:51:57.550 --> 00:52:00.317
If we're going to regard
our CTS as being extremely,

00:52:00.317 --> 00:52:02.010
you know, if we're going to use language

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:05.770
like gold standards,
because we think, you know,

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:08.610
all the con founders
are randomly assigned.

00:52:08.610 --> 00:52:09.980
They're not effecting things, right.

00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:13.360
And of course you can't really
get ever prove that it could

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:14.900
be the case that there are confines that

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:16.790
you're not measuring and
they weren't randomly,

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:18.420
they didn't happen to balance out, right?

00:52:18.420 --> 00:52:20.640
Randomization probably balances it open

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:23.093
it doesn't ascertain it for sure.

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:26.550
But I think the thing
to focus on even more

00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:28.430
is about this again,

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:31.420
what is the purpose of the
randomized control trial?

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:35.590
And in what respect does
it not suffice for purposes

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:38.620
that actually we deem
very important, right?

00:52:38.620 --> 00:52:41.810
So the RCT is answering a
different question altogether.

00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:43.110
It's answering this question.

00:52:43.110 --> 00:52:45.240
If, for example, in the context of a drug,

00:52:45.240 --> 00:52:47.360
does this drug work
under perfect conditions?

00:52:47.360 --> 00:52:48.570
That's the question.

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:51.590
And at some point, the relevance
of that question expires,

00:52:51.590 --> 00:52:53.800
and we're not interested in that anymore.

00:52:53.800 --> 00:52:56.450
We're interested about
whether it's working for us

00:52:56.450 --> 00:52:57.350
in the real world.

00:52:57.350 --> 00:53:00.160
And then we really get
into a lot of challenges

00:53:00.160 --> 00:53:01.650
because we're into this.

00:53:01.650 --> 00:53:03.150
Well, now we're in the
real world and there's

00:53:03.150 --> 00:53:04.640
all sorts of crazy stuff going on

00:53:04.640 --> 00:53:06.655
and we don't know what
we have to control for.

00:53:06.655 --> 00:53:10.660
And it's very different
types of folks who are taking

00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:13.982
those drugs and we lose
track of, you know,

00:53:13.982 --> 00:53:16.440
are they taking it as prescribed?

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:18.870
Are they interacting maybe and
other things they're taking

00:53:18.870 --> 00:53:22.725
at the same time and so
on and so on and so on.

00:53:22.725 --> 00:53:25.390
So yeah, I think it really does underscore

00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:27.640
that we have to understand
the different purposes,

00:53:27.640 --> 00:53:30.236
and as well we have to
understand our limitations.

00:53:30.236 --> 00:53:32.990
Because it's true that
once you bump into that

00:53:32.990 --> 00:53:36.690
observational study
context, then you're facing

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:38.140
all the problems that, you know, Eric

00:53:38.140 --> 00:53:40.120
and I just went through in terms of.

00:53:40.120 --> 00:53:41.593
- Yup.

00:53:41.593 --> 00:53:43.106
- Well now you don't have
that benefit of at least

00:53:43.106 --> 00:53:43.939
randomization seems effective

00:53:43.939 --> 00:53:45.803
for maybe balancing out compounds.

00:53:47.190 --> 00:53:48.610
Eric.

00:53:48.610 --> 00:53:49.443
- Just real quick.

00:53:49.443 --> 00:53:53.473
I mean, this just merely
summarizing what Stephanie said.

00:53:53.473 --> 00:53:54.750
But look, randomized control is great.

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:56.820
It's a great thing to be able to do,

00:53:56.820 --> 00:53:59.300
but it also imposes costs like,

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:01.530
suppose you want to do
a randomized control

00:54:01.530 --> 00:54:04.530
on the effectiveness of masks, right?

00:54:04.530 --> 00:54:05.675
You could do that.

00:54:05.675 --> 00:54:07.620
Let's say, you've got
around certain ethical,

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:08.950
you know, issues on that.

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:09.783
You did it.

00:54:09.783 --> 00:54:10.710
What would that look like?

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:12.000
Presumably that would look like,

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:13.360
you'd get a bunch of people,

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:15.690
you'd make some of them
wear masks and, you know,

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:19.020
send them into certain controlled
conditions and then see

00:54:19.020 --> 00:54:21.200
how they compare to the people
that didn't wear the masks.

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:22.570
Now you're going to eliminate

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:24.350
a lot of compounds there, right?

00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:26.350
You probably eliminate all the compounds,

00:54:26.350 --> 00:54:29.060
but you're looking at a very
narrow set of circumstances.

00:54:29.060 --> 00:54:32.310
You're not then seeing whether,
you know, you're getting,

00:54:32.310 --> 00:54:35.760
you're just determining what
mask use does in this very

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:38.280
narrow constrained set of circumstances

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:39.640
that your trial is taking place.

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.221
And she might not be that
informative about what happens

00:54:42.221 --> 00:54:44.870
when, you know, people
are expected to wear masks

00:54:44.870 --> 00:54:47.020
for eight hour shifts
or children are asked

00:54:47.020 --> 00:54:50.550
to wear masks in schools or,
you know, excited, sorry.

00:54:50.550 --> 00:54:55.500
You can't possibly do on RCT
in which all of the, you know,

00:54:55.500 --> 00:54:59.963
sort of in the wild
phenomenon are being studied.

00:54:59.963 --> 00:55:00.827
- Right.

00:55:00.827 --> 00:55:03.450
- So observational studies do
have a number of advantages

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:07.380
over RCTs because they do
take place in the wild.

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:09.740
But of course, then they
have all their compounds,

00:55:09.740 --> 00:55:10.573
et cetera, et cetera.

00:55:10.573 --> 00:55:12.050
So there's no magic bullet.

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:13.850
- Right, and I think that, as you said

00:55:13.850 --> 00:55:16.550
in this kind of mask study
example that you just gave.

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:19.908
I mean, the idea that
you are marginalizing

00:55:19.908 --> 00:55:24.908
over all other variables,
even in controlled study

00:55:25.416 --> 00:55:28.819
is clearly observed because
two different people

00:55:28.819 --> 00:55:31.914
with different genetic physiological,

00:55:31.914 --> 00:55:36.500
underlying proclivities
conditions are going

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:40.610
to react differently to
the same level of exposure

00:55:40.610 --> 00:55:42.010
or lack there off, right?

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:45.330
And so we know that
already, we know that about

00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:50.220
all our bodies, that there
isn't one metric that can

00:55:51.690 --> 00:55:56.690
represent any kind of risk X
because the marginalization

00:55:56.890 --> 00:56:00.430
over all other variables
just doesn't work.

00:56:00.430 --> 00:56:02.550
I mean, it really doesn't work.

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:04.860
I think we should move on to
the questions that I would like

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:09.470
to first see the floor to sort
of two questions from Chris

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:13.410
who has been engaging
with us offline as well.

00:56:13.410 --> 00:56:17.590
And so he says that he has
two questions that are similar

00:56:17.590 --> 00:56:20.850
to the third question
that Stephanie raised.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:23.410
Which first that policymakers are faced

00:56:23.410 --> 00:56:25.130
with a comparative assessment.

00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:28.380
How do we compare models with
other sources of evidence

00:56:28.380 --> 00:56:31.700
regarding the effects
of policy interventions?

00:56:31.700 --> 00:56:33.270
They could still be the best

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:35.990
of a very bad lot of possibilities.

00:56:35.990 --> 00:56:39.180
Second looking forward,
how could we do better

00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:41.976
at drawing these kinds
of causal inferences?

00:56:41.976 --> 00:56:45.410
Are there different kinds
of data that public health

00:56:45.410 --> 00:56:47.663
agencies should be gathering for example?

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:53.950
- So I'll start with the sec.

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:56.790
Yeah, I'll start with the second question,

00:56:56.790 --> 00:56:58.743
because I think that's a little easier.

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:01.950
So to the second question,

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:04.880
I think there are two things
that I've been sort of

00:57:04.880 --> 00:57:08.080
pressing for over the last several months.

00:57:08.080 --> 00:57:10.470
One is I don't think that we,

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:12.250
I think we did too much changing our mind

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:15.780
about the received wisdom over
the course of this pandemic.

00:57:15.780 --> 00:57:20.320
There were, you know, doing
a number of, you know,

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:24.130
sort of standard procedures
in the CDC for doing things.

00:57:24.130 --> 00:57:26.580
And they said things like,
you know, don't close schools

00:57:26.580 --> 00:57:31.050
for more than six weeks,
you know, et cetera.

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:33.770
And we very quickly
decided in this pandemic,

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:37.070
I think in part, because we
were watching what looked like

00:57:37.070 --> 00:57:38.770
we thought we knew it
was going on in China,

00:57:38.770 --> 00:57:40.260
we thought like this is so different.

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:42.019
We need to just rip up a playbook

00:57:42.019 --> 00:57:45.490
and we need to just be making
all these wild inferences on

00:57:45.490 --> 00:57:47.700
the fly and guiding policy with.

00:57:47.700 --> 00:57:50.890
And I don't think that was super wise.

00:57:50.890 --> 00:57:53.770
I think, you know, science is done,

00:57:53.770 --> 00:57:55.920
good science is done slowly.

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:58.990
And it's done, you know,
with peer criticism

00:57:58.990 --> 00:58:02.510
and it's done with
stakeholders having a voice.

00:58:02.510 --> 00:58:05.000
It's so that was part of it.

00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:06.370
And then, yeah, I mean,

00:58:06.370 --> 00:58:08.700
early on also very early on the pandemic,

00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:10.880
I think we were doing a lot
of modeling was very poor

00:58:10.880 --> 00:58:14.900
information about what,
you know, the outcome

00:58:14.900 --> 00:58:17.900
of the virus was in different
age groups, et cetera.

00:58:17.900 --> 00:58:20.550
And that bear that was the element

00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:21.670
that was an easy fix, right?

00:58:21.670 --> 00:58:25.517
We could have been doing
a lot more, you know,

00:58:25.517 --> 00:58:28.660
serology testing to figure out.

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:30.940
Well, you know, how many
people have been infected?

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:31.773
It's kind of a scandal.

00:58:31.773 --> 00:58:33.994
I think that I just, you know,

00:58:33.994 --> 00:58:37.510
I obsess over this stuff and I
don't really have a good feel

00:58:37.510 --> 00:58:40.978
for how many Americans have
been infected with COVID.

00:58:40.978 --> 00:58:42.990
I think it's probably
somewhere between 100 million

00:58:42.990 --> 00:58:47.100
and 150 million, but that's probably

00:58:47.100 --> 00:58:49.240
the uncertainty gap is wider than that.

00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:51.450
That's where I put my and already

00:58:51.450 --> 00:58:53.050
that's appallingly large.

00:58:53.050 --> 00:58:54.150
We just should've done more than that.

00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:55.937
We could have known more than that.

00:58:55.937 --> 00:58:57.950
We could've been doing
a lot more spot testing,

00:58:57.950 --> 00:59:00.620
random spot testing and
doing more cirrhotic testing,

00:59:00.620 --> 00:59:01.867
getting a better feel for
how many people have been

00:59:01.867 --> 00:59:04.100
affected, which would have given us early

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:08.217
on a much better sense
of what the, you know,

00:59:08.217 --> 00:59:11.240
danger of the rash placed
on different age groups,

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:14.920
trying to which my past adopting
quite different policies

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:15.930
with protective schools.

00:59:15.930 --> 00:59:17.610
I think in the developed world,

00:59:17.610 --> 00:59:20.300
I think our, the way we've
handled schools has been,

00:59:20.300 --> 00:59:21.310
you know, pretty appalling.

00:59:21.310 --> 00:59:23.880
There was a really good
piece in the New York times

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:26.560
last week that I'll try
to share at some point.

00:59:26.560 --> 00:59:28.942
I think everybody should read
just about how scandalous,

00:59:28.942 --> 00:59:32.870
the way we've handled
schools in this country

00:59:32.870 --> 00:59:36.030
has been over the last year and a half.

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:38.220
So yeah, so that's,

00:59:38.220 --> 00:59:40.010
I think that was your
second question, Chris.

00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:42.880
Your first question was,
oh right, what do we do

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:46.230
when it's the best out
of a bad group of walls?

00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:48.820
I think the sketch to
Stephanie's question about like,

00:59:48.820 --> 00:59:51.570
when does research have
negative expected utility?

00:59:51.570 --> 00:59:53.420
And I could answer certainly as sometimes.

00:59:53.420 --> 00:59:57.600
I think sometimes the best
science out of, you know,

00:59:57.600 --> 01:00:00.490
a bad lot might just be
better left, not done,

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:05.490
or at least not paraded around, right?

01:00:06.640 --> 01:00:11.640
In a way that suggests it's,
you know, I mean, you know,

01:00:12.290 --> 01:00:16.300
we've had a lot of rhetoric
in the last year and a half

01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:19.690
about how various policies
are following the science.

01:00:19.690 --> 01:00:23.010
I mean, that's really makes
it sound like two things.

01:00:23.010 --> 01:00:24.750
One that there's very little
uncertainty in the science,

01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:26.650
and that was crazy.

01:00:26.650 --> 01:00:28.540
And second that there's like, you know,

01:00:28.540 --> 01:00:31.960
some deterministic mapping
from like the outside

01:00:31.960 --> 01:00:34.631
of the sciences to like
what the right policies are.

01:00:34.631 --> 01:00:35.810
And that's also crazy.

01:00:35.810 --> 01:00:39.993
So yeah, I don't know
when you got, you know,

01:00:40.827 --> 01:00:44.063
the least bad method
available, what should you do?

01:00:44.063 --> 01:00:46.200
I think that's a tough question.

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:50.130
And I think when that happens, you know,

01:00:50.130 --> 01:00:51.910
all of the things Stephanie
was talking about.

01:00:51.910 --> 01:00:55.400
About public participation
and about, you know, inquiry.

01:00:55.400 --> 01:00:58.556
I think the more, you know,
uncertainty you have, the more

01:00:58.556 --> 01:01:02.170
you're selecting from, you
know, the least bad model

01:01:02.170 --> 01:01:04.083
from the least bad method
from about bad methods,

01:01:05.486 --> 01:01:09.339
the more you need to be paying
attention to the fact that

01:01:09.339 --> 01:01:14.339
you're in grave danger of
you know, putting forward

01:01:17.430 --> 01:01:20.535
both knowledge and
recommendations of policies

01:01:20.535 --> 01:01:23.457
that favor some stakeholders over others.

01:01:32.650 --> 01:01:33.910
- Thank you.

01:01:33.910 --> 01:01:35.973
Stephanie, did you want to add anything?

01:01:40.389 --> 01:01:43.520
- I think I've lost lost the
nugget that I would reply to.

01:01:43.520 --> 01:01:45.500
So maybe we should move on to the next one

01:01:45.500 --> 01:01:46.810
and I'll weigh in on that one.

01:01:46.810 --> 01:01:47.850
- Okay, great.

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:52.850
So the next question was
by Sanji Kumar on causal

01:01:53.450 --> 01:01:57.490
discovery and the extent to
which it is about the search

01:01:57.490 --> 01:01:59.130
for right counterfactuals

01:01:59.130 --> 01:02:02.120
and that it is largely a social process.

01:02:02.120 --> 01:02:04.430
So the role of mentors, communities,

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:06.390
connections and networks.

01:02:06.390 --> 01:02:09.710
So is this social learning
aspect that does not get

01:02:09.710 --> 01:02:13.470
sufficiently emphasized as a
way nonlinearity gets factored

01:02:13.470 --> 01:02:16.736
in, and this may not be
amenable to modeling at all,

01:02:16.736 --> 01:02:19.360
but how, but we do need to acknowledge it.

01:02:19.360 --> 01:02:21.890
And what are your thoughts

01:02:21.890 --> 01:02:25.483
about how to take that into account?

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:30.090
- I guess I'm not exactly familiar

01:02:30.090 --> 01:02:33.533
with what the question is talking about.

01:02:34.400 --> 01:02:35.300
Stephanie is that.

01:02:36.308 --> 01:02:39.360
- I'm going to take a
stab at replying to that.

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:41.570
And if I'm off the mark, just let me know.

01:02:41.570 --> 01:02:44.990
I also I'm unfamiliar with
some of the terms in that

01:02:44.990 --> 01:02:47.550
question, but the part
that I think I'm picking up

01:02:47.550 --> 01:02:50.450
is really important has to do
with the fact that you do not

01:02:50.450 --> 01:02:52.700
have to model every question.

01:02:52.700 --> 01:02:55.250
And I think that's exactly right.

01:02:55.250 --> 01:02:58.390
And I think that like part
of what really interests me

01:02:58.390 --> 01:03:01.450
is having more open
conversations about like,

01:03:01.450 --> 01:03:05.580
what is it that we want to
model, like when we talk about

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:08.840
ourselves as a community
and so far as it's possible,

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:12.540
like which questions do which
communities want to approach

01:03:12.540 --> 01:03:14.170
with a modeling strategy?

01:03:14.170 --> 01:03:18.000
I think it's wrong to be
pretending that every single

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:20.800
question has to be
approached in this fashion.

01:03:20.800 --> 01:03:22.860
And it does connect to
what Eric's talking about

01:03:22.860 --> 01:03:24.760
in terms of the negative expected utility

01:03:24.760 --> 01:03:27.470
of research in certain contexts, right?

01:03:27.470 --> 01:03:31.830
So for example, I think
sometimes we find ourselves

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:34.410
facing a problem that it is very new.

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:36.428
It's going to take some
time to figure out,

01:03:36.428 --> 01:03:39.500
and maybe we do want to
assign some scientists

01:03:39.500 --> 01:03:41.570
the role of slowly figuring that out.

01:03:41.570 --> 01:03:44.384
But that doesn't mean that
we don't have to figure out

01:03:44.384 --> 01:03:47.400
policies for now for the interim, right?

01:03:47.400 --> 01:03:50.397
And I think that it would be
okay in some context to say,

01:03:50.397 --> 01:03:53.270
okay, as a community,
what are we going to do?

01:03:53.270 --> 01:03:55.340
We don't have good information right now.

01:03:55.340 --> 01:03:56.510
Let's acknowledge that.

01:03:56.510 --> 01:04:01.070
And let's just think about what
our goals are and see if we

01:04:01.070 --> 01:04:03.590
can get on the same page
about what we really want to

01:04:03.590 --> 01:04:06.840
achieve and whether we can
come up with strategies to

01:04:06.840 --> 01:04:09.860
achieve those goals that
allow us to avail ourselves of

01:04:09.860 --> 01:04:12.990
information that we think is
really good quality, okay?

01:04:12.990 --> 01:04:16.410
And I'm thinking in the
context of health, like there's

01:04:16.410 --> 01:04:19.050
a whole debate about what
health even is, right?

01:04:19.050 --> 01:04:23.400
And if we want to achieve
like in COVID extensively,

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:24.980
what people are really worried about

01:04:24.980 --> 01:04:27.010
is people's health, right?

01:04:27.010 --> 01:04:29.990
Well, there are lots of things
that society drops the ball

01:04:29.990 --> 01:04:34.830
on all the time in terms of
failing people's health, right?

01:04:34.830 --> 01:04:36.600
But that doesn't mean
they're because we have poor

01:04:36.600 --> 01:04:39.210
information about how to
maximize people's health, right?

01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:42.130
So without having and don't get me wrong,

01:04:42.130 --> 01:04:44.220
I'm not trying to take this too, too far,

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:48.280
but there are simple things
that society wasn't doing prior

01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:50.258
to COVID anyway.

01:04:50.258 --> 01:04:54.660
And it might've made sense in
some more moral conversations

01:04:54.660 --> 01:04:57.000
to say, okay, well we
have a pandemic going on.

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:00.500
What are some strategies
that probably regardless of

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:03.460
uncertainty, we could
feel good about pursuing?

01:05:03.460 --> 01:05:07.580
Could we feel good about pursuing
more sick days for people?

01:05:07.580 --> 01:05:10.120
I think you could come up
with a conversation that was

01:05:10.120 --> 01:05:12.910
really just normative
in nature that said yes,

01:05:12.910 --> 01:05:13.743
in this context,

01:05:13.743 --> 01:05:16.410
I think we can feel good
about investing in sick days.

01:05:16.410 --> 01:05:20.680
Maybe we can good about investing
in strategies to make sure

01:05:20.680 --> 01:05:23.830
there's not overcrowding in certain areas.

01:05:23.830 --> 01:05:25.930
So those are just some
questions that I would bring up,

01:05:25.930 --> 01:05:29.350
like in terms of if we
in some contexts want

01:05:29.350 --> 01:05:32.050
to shy away from modeling,
what would replace it?

01:05:32.050 --> 01:05:36.383
I think some public
deliberation could go some ways.

01:05:37.700 --> 01:05:39.073
- Okay, now, thank you.

01:05:40.840 --> 01:05:44.660
I wanted to move on to the next question,

01:05:44.660 --> 01:05:46.970
which I'm going to have
to interpret a little bit

01:05:46.970 --> 01:05:51.300
and that I think has to
do with how do we build

01:05:51.300 --> 01:05:53.823
and construct epistemic trust?

01:05:58.350 --> 01:06:00.520
- Yeah, we got a lot of bridges to fix.

01:06:00.520 --> 01:06:02.590
I think about epistemic trust.

01:06:02.590 --> 01:06:05.390
I think, you know, look, part
of the reason I brought up

01:06:05.390 --> 01:06:07.900
some of these somewhat silly

01:06:07.900 --> 01:06:10.933
causal inferences yesterday,
things like, you know,

01:06:13.250 --> 01:06:18.130
that the motorcycle rally
caused 35% of the cases

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:20.720
in the United States over
the course of two weeks that,

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:24.760
you know, that Trump voting
counties were the ones

01:06:24.760 --> 01:06:26.688
where there were the most COVID cases,

01:06:26.688 --> 01:06:29.137
et cetera, et cetera, is, I mean,

01:06:29.137 --> 01:06:33.290
this is incredibly dead that
people aren't stupid, right?

01:06:33.290 --> 01:06:35.550
I mean, you know, people aren't rubes.

01:06:35.550 --> 01:06:38.620
People know that the walls
being pulled over their eyes

01:06:38.620 --> 01:06:40.240
with stuff like that.

01:06:40.240 --> 01:06:42.700
And it's incredibly, I think,

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:46.900
damaging just scientific
trust to engage in that stuff.

01:06:46.900 --> 01:06:49.610
And I it's, you know, I,
it was an election year.

01:06:49.610 --> 01:06:54.070
People were passionate about
this last election for reasons.

01:06:54.070 --> 01:06:58.330
I understand very well,
but it was alarming to me.

01:06:58.330 --> 01:06:59.980
I think that people were
willing to sort of throw

01:06:59.980 --> 01:07:03.290
a scientific trust under the bus for that.

01:07:03.290 --> 01:07:04.270
I don't know what we can do.

01:07:04.270 --> 01:07:08.450
I don't know what we can do to repair it.

01:07:08.450 --> 01:07:10.100
Stephanie, you have any thoughts?

01:07:11.340 --> 01:07:15.990
- Yeah, I mean, I guess
I do, I feel somewhat.

01:07:15.990 --> 01:07:18.546
Well, I don't know if
optimistic is the right word,

01:07:18.546 --> 01:07:21.770
but I think one of the
strategies to invest in is more

01:07:21.770 --> 01:07:23.790
public participation in science.

01:07:23.790 --> 01:07:26.460
Of course, this has been said
by many, many, many people,

01:07:26.460 --> 01:07:28.710
but I think that it takes
on an additional importance

01:07:28.710 --> 01:07:30.930
when it comes to this
really complex modeling

01:07:30.930 --> 01:07:32.320
that's going on.

01:07:32.320 --> 01:07:34.850
I happen to work in
more than one initiative

01:07:34.850 --> 01:07:36.530
that this is the goal, right?

01:07:36.530 --> 01:07:39.012
Is talking to folks
about modeling going on.

01:07:39.012 --> 01:07:41.280
And I've met a lot of people who,

01:07:41.280 --> 01:07:43.020
the knowledge isn't even there,

01:07:43.020 --> 01:07:45.750
that those are techniques
that are being used, right?

01:07:45.750 --> 01:07:47.790
So I don't think that's right.

01:07:47.790 --> 01:07:51.440
I think that they have a huge
influence on society and that

01:07:51.440 --> 01:07:55.390
it's not right for science as
an establishment to sort of

01:07:55.390 --> 01:07:58.700
just take off at high speeds and do

01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:00.530
a lot of this problem solving,

01:08:00.530 --> 01:08:03.835
using these strategies without
making that additional effort

01:08:03.835 --> 01:08:06.740
to engage with publics
and say, hey, you know,

01:08:06.740 --> 01:08:08.450
we have these strategies that we're using

01:08:08.450 --> 01:08:09.480
and this is how they work.

01:08:09.480 --> 01:08:12.340
And one of the things that I
think is important is that when

01:08:12.340 --> 01:08:16.450
you're given a chance to
explain your methods to lots of

01:08:16.450 --> 01:08:20.080
different people at lots of
different levels, you know,

01:08:20.080 --> 01:08:21.610
you can actually force yourself

01:08:21.610 --> 01:08:23.300
to look at some challenges, right?

01:08:23.300 --> 01:08:24.840
Because you can get some tough questions.

01:08:24.840 --> 01:08:26.940
Oh, well, how do you show this?

01:08:26.940 --> 01:08:28.530
Oh, well actually we can't.

01:08:28.530 --> 01:08:30.280
You know, we have to
rely on this assumption

01:08:30.280 --> 01:08:31.320
or that assumption.

01:08:31.320 --> 01:08:33.920
And those are the types of
conversations that, I mean,

01:08:33.920 --> 01:08:35.170
I don't know, I'm not an expert

01:08:35.170 --> 01:08:37.900
in what exactly builds
trust, but in speaking

01:08:37.900 --> 01:08:41.547
from my own personal perspective,
when I talk to scientists,

01:08:41.547 --> 01:08:45.100
I really want to be able to
know that they can tell me

01:08:45.100 --> 01:08:47.840
what it is that they're
doing and be honest

01:08:47.840 --> 01:08:50.160
about like, where is it?

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:51.480
Are we having to make assumptions?

01:08:51.480 --> 01:08:52.950
And what are the gaps and so on.

01:08:52.950 --> 01:08:55.500
So I think there's a huge push to be very,

01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:57.130
very fast about a lot of things.

01:08:57.130 --> 01:08:59.810
And I think some of the solution
is probably down the road

01:08:59.810 --> 01:09:01.243
of being a bit slower.

01:09:02.380 --> 01:09:05.130
- Yeah, thank you.

01:09:05.130 --> 01:09:07.593
So moving on to the next question,

01:09:09.061 --> 01:09:11.100
the question is that they want to,

